                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
                        "entry_id": "53c05eb8-58d2-3d19-8999-e531e0902357",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Femme (personne de genre féminin)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "192d8674-6867-43c5-b5e3-76c18daf902a",
                                "sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
                                "text": "Vous êtes DJ et vous vous identifiez comme une personne fxmme, une personne BIPOC et/ou un.e membre de la communauté 2SLGTBQIA+ En plus de jouer de la musique incroyable? Cet appel est pour vous!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "56664cb8-5dfe-4ae5-9915-c30a4b1e44d6",
                                "sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
                                "text": "Ce 8 mars, Journée internationale de la femme, l'ISP célèbre un tournant dans la lutte pour les fxmme dans le monde entier. Il ne suffit plus de parler de politiques sensibles au genre ou répondant aux besoins des femmes, nous exigeons une transformation et des actions politiques qui démantèlent les causes structurelles de l'inégalité."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1fb92090-4016-4be1-85d5-dbc571ae0b39",
                                "sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
                                "text": "Vous êtes DJ et vous vous identifiez comme une personne fxmme, une personne BIPOC et/ou un.e membre de la communauté 2SLGTBQIA+ En plus de jouer de la musique incroyable? Cet appel est pour vous!"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "85d4660a-9ac1-4cad-88d6-b4a5bcfb78a2",
                        "source_entry_id": "53c05eb8-58d2-3d19-8999-e531e0902357",
                        "source_sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
                        "target_lang_word": "womxn",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "womxn",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d3a94cbb-8ad2-350a-975b-0bb00aa3a643",
                                        "entry_id": "6d1c4e48-b26e-3a70-8d71-b197d014f43f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Feminist spelling of woman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7813a371-79d8-42da-8322-a2a823d9a226",
                                                "sense_id": "d3a94cbb-8ad2-350a-975b-0bb00aa3a643",
                                                "text": "A womxn whose ability to carry out this work is through the grace of Anishinaabe mxn and institutions? A womxn without a grove of birch whose bark may be harvested without fear of negative consequences?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7baff5ab-f11c-4f2b-8ca3-5ebf0690d7fd",
                                                "sense_id": "d3a94cbb-8ad2-350a-975b-0bb00aa3a643",
                                                "text": "“Coyolxauhqui Canto” by Cristina Gorocica, a song written in offering to the moon, documents the moment of recognition when a womxn's body undergoing a metamorphoses to accommodate the growth of life is mirrored back in the phases of [the moon]."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "womxn",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8d6b067b-5492-3246-aae4-73d35bd239d6",
                                        "entry_id": "dcb5108c-02b6-3637-99f0-a5c0ee7e6d06",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Feminist spelling of women."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6eebadcb-b9aa-4cc3-bd88-d2369757f48b",
                                                "sense_id": "8d6b067b-5492-3246-aae4-73d35bd239d6",
                                                "text": "To every person I spoke with on my many trips across the country, to guides/mentors/friends/fam: to Alexis Pauline Gumbs, for living queer Black brilliance and Black love, for creating space to remember Harriett Tubman, and for continuing to create Black Spaces for us rooted in the ways and words of Black womxn; ..."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "05401ca4-3ba5-4bdd-a537-e523d0dc0bda",
                                                "sense_id": "8d6b067b-5492-3246-aae4-73d35bd239d6",
                                                "text": "Twenty-One Ways of Leaving:\n[…]\n19. The womxn in their thirties.\nThe womxn with wrinkles at the edges of their eyes.\nThe womxn who spell lonely with ambition.\nThe womxn in their thirties.\nThe womxn who do not bring up your age at the dinner table.\nThe womxn who bring up your accolades at the dinner table.\nThe womxn who make love to you\nand introduce you at the dinner table\nlike they have not been intimate with your sheets.\nOh honey, run.\nNo.\nThe other way."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8892a99c-e634-4aa0-b9df-f3e99d6c015e",
                                                "sense_id": "8d6b067b-5492-3246-aae4-73d35bd239d6",
                                                "text": "She was born in Zihuatanejo, Guerrero, Mexico, and migrated with her family in the early 1990s to the Pacific Northwest. She is also a hip-hop artist with Batallones Femeninos, a womxn's hip-hop collective based in Mexico, and does multimedia production and autonomous organizing with Shades of Silence. She is currently in the process of applying to film school and to a graduate doctoral program to extend her work of supporting womxn and Indigenous communities ..."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3821722f-e043-4db8-a13c-258f2d305678",
                        "source_entry_id": "53c05eb8-58d2-3d19-8999-e531e0902357",
                        "source_sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
                        "target_lang_word": "womyn",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "womyn",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0dc16087-bc6c-3baf-b7b8-8896a0a63079",
                                        "entry_id": "4a00bfa2-9fb0-3d8d-8d98-29b29dd5b9df",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Feminist spelling of woman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd572d43-40e2-487f-9389-f6c5e08d43f7",
                                                "sense_id": "0dc16087-bc6c-3baf-b7b8-8896a0a63079",
                                                "text": "Summoning all my courage, I touched and kissed a womyn for the first time. I cannot describe what I felt at that moment."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c755a6c-b6d1-4e93-ad4d-f84b29be9bbc",
                                                "sense_id": "0dc16087-bc6c-3baf-b7b8-8896a0a63079",
                                                "text": "I dug with my bare hands through the bullshit where beneath I discovered a womyn. She was an incredibly angry womyn, but a womyn nonetheless."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "41adc677-3ced-32eb-88bb-e3b7d35762df",
                                        "entry_id": "4a00bfa2-9fb0-3d8d-8d98-29b29dd5b9df",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Feminist spelling of women."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a8ec0a81-0cd2-4567-be0e-24071f2cbad8",
                                                "sense_id": "41adc677-3ced-32eb-88bb-e3b7d35762df",
                                                "text": "And I'm sure you know that so many straight womyn are so miserable that \"going to the shrink\" has become their major occupation, if they can afford it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "70c5e2cc-6beb-44a1-863d-636f0446680b",
                                                "sense_id": "41adc677-3ced-32eb-88bb-e3b7d35762df",
                                                "text": "Likewise, simply dismissing post-operative male-to-female transsexuals as \"men,\" and making an artificial distinction between \"women\" and \"womyn\" neither justifies their policy, nor eliminates any of the substantive issues involved."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4874489e-a417-40ef-9c76-06952104a097",
                                                "sense_id": "41adc677-3ced-32eb-88bb-e3b7d35762df",
                                                "text": "Since the publication of the Random House Webster's College Dictionary (RHWCD) in the spring of 1991, no single dictionary entry has been the subject of more discussion, criticism, and controversy than the entry womyn (an alternative spelling of women)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f62a647c-cf6f-4def-b689-f7e454c15e31",
                        "source_entry_id": "53c05eb8-58d2-3d19-8999-e531e0902357",
                        "source_sense_id": "3e9d9789-9d66-355a-8bf9-5ea93bb80103",
